рина said tests pass. Let me think about whether the program output has a trailing newline. show_format = `std::format(\"{:.3f} {:.3f} {:.3f}\\n\", ...)`. So it writes \"...\\n\". When stdout is redirected to a file in text mode (default on Windows), \\n → \\r\\n. So output file = \"4.000 5.000 5.000\\r\\n\" = 19 bytes.\n\nExpect t4_test2 = 17 bytes = \"4.000 5.000 5.000\" (no trailing CRLF).\n\nFC would report difference because of the trailing CRLF! Unless... the tests actually fail, or unless the output has no CRLF. Hmm. Wait — maybe she runs the tests and they pass. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually — wait. Let me check: is stdout in text mode or binary mode? By default, the C runtime opens stdout in text mode on Windows. When redirected to a file, \\n → \\r\\n. So the output file should have \\r\\n.\n\nBut FC — does it compare CRLF-insensitively? No, FC compares byte-by-byte (well, it ignores trailing... actually FC by default does NOT ignore differences, it reports all). Actually `fc` compares line by line and reports differences. If one file has trailing \\r\\n and other doesn't, FC typically reports a difference (extra line or last line difference).\n\nHmm. But she said tests pass. Let me actually check whether the output file exists in build and what its bytes are. Wait — plan mode, read-only. I can READ the build output files! Let me check `build\\debug\\t4_filter_cpp\\t4_test2`.\n\nActually, this is important to verify. Let me read the actual output file and compare to expect. This is read-only.\n\nAlso I should check FC definition and whether the test actually ran (the .ok file). Let me look.\n\nActually — hmm, maybe the issue: FC is `fc` which by default compares... let me recall. `FC` behavior: \"fc file1 file2\" — compares two files. It's line-based? Actually `fc` is byte-based by default for binary files, but for text files it's line-based with options. Hmm, actually FC compares line by line and reports differences. Trailing whitespace differences ARE reported as differences.\n\nLet me just check the actual build output.\n\n**Issue K: `.PHONY` includes t4, `all` includes t4, `test` includes T4_CPP_TESTS.** But note line 539-540: test includes T4_CPP_TESTS but NOT T4_C_TESTS — because C version of t4 isn't done yet (T4_C_EXE defined at line 72 but no tests). Consistent with \"t4 c++ tests\". Fine. But note `all: t1 t2 t3 t4` and `t4: $(T4_CPP_EXE) $(T4_C_EXE)` — so `make all` requires T4_C_EXE too! If the C version isn't finished, `make all` would try to build it. Let me check: T4_C_EXE := build\\debug\\t4_filter_c\\main.exe, and rule at line 245. t4_filter_c/main.c — does it exist? Let me check. If it compiles, fine.\n\nLet me also check FC variable definition and T4_C sources.\n\nLet me do the read-only checks.",
