text files use CRLF as line terminator; Unix uses LF only. So \"при перенаправлении stdout в файл CRT в текстовом режиме\" means: when the program's output is redirected to a file, the C runtime, operating in text mode by default, inserts \\r before each \\n, so the file on disk contains CRLF line endings.\n\nAnd fc in text mode: fc compares line-by-line ignoring line-terminator details, so the trailing CRLF vs none doesn't count as a difference. Only /b catches it.\n\nNow question 3: nan = not a number. She thought nan = not a number, so the test name t4_cpp_nan_point... The input \"2 wow 3\" contains a non-numeric token \"wow\". The error message is \"Строка 1. Нечисловое значение: 2 wow 3\". So it's a \"non-numeric value\" case, not a NaN case. NaN specifically is a floating-point value (0/0, sqrt(-1)) that from_chars actually parses as a number (it parses \"nan\"!). So naming: better \"t4_cpp_non_numeric_coords\" or \"t4_cpp_bad_coords\" or \"t4_cpp_symbol_coords\". She suggested \"points symbol\" — maybe \"t4_cpp_symbol_coords\" or \"t4_cpp_non_digit_coords\". Let me think about what's most descriptive and consistent with existing names.\n\nExisting names: t4_cpp_nan_point, t4_cpp_too_few_coords, t4_cpp_empty_coords. So a good rename: t4_cpp_non_numeric_coords or t4_cpp_symbol_coords. Actually in t3 there was \"not_digit_case\". So consistent would be t4_cpp_not_digit_case or t4_cpp_non_numeric_case. Hmm. t1 used \"abc\" (t1_cpp_abc.ok). t3 used not_digit. So maybe t4_cpp_symbol_case or t4_cpp_not_digit_case. I'll suggest and ask.\n\nWait — also worth noting: a real NaN coordinate IS a valid parse for from_chars (\"nan\" string parses as NaN floating point). In the C version, strtod also parses \"nan\". So the point with NaN coordinate would pass parse_point, then hypot(NaN...) = NaN, comparison NaN < R is false (NaN comparisons are always false), so the point would NOT be output. Interesting edge case but not what this test covers. This test covers non-numeric token. So rename is appropriate.\n\nLet me now also confirm the fc text-mode behavior in my explanation. I have empirical proof: fc default → 0, fc /b → 1. \n\nLet me write the answer in Russian, concise but thorough. This is a conceptual Q&A in plan mode — no edits.\n\nI should explain:\n1. CRT, CRLF, text mode, and why output file has CRLF.\n2. Why fc passes: fc default = text mode comparison (line-based), ignores trailing newline; /b = byte mode catches it. Show the empirical result.\n3. Naming: nan = Not a Number is a specific IEEE float value; the test is about non-numeric token. Suggest rename.\n\nLet me also double check the fc text mode semantics more precisely: fc compares files and the docs say it does an \"ASCII mode\" comparison by default for .exe? Actually fc has /a and /b. By default fc does a binary compare? No — the docs: \"fc [options] file1 file2\". By default fc compares in ASCII (text) mode. /b does binary. Hmm, actually I recall default is ASCII text comparison which is line-oriented. The empirical result shows default = 0 (identical), /b = 1 (different). So my claim stands: default fc is text/line-oriented and insensitive to trailing EOL.\n\nActually, let me be careful about fc's default. Microsoft docs: \"Compares two files or sets of files and displays the differences between them.\" Options: /a ASCII comparison, /b binary comparison, /c case-insensitive, /l line number display, /n, /t, /u, /w, /lb n. If you don't specify, fc does... I believe it auto-detects or does ASCII. The observed behavior: default says no differences. So default treats the CRLF vs no-CRLF as not-a-difference. That's the key teaching point.\n\nAlso: text mode fc ignores more than trailing newline? It might also ignore \\r differences. In ASCII mode, fc compares lines, and line terminators... Actually I shouldn't overclaim; I'll say: in its default (text) mode fc compares by lines and does not treat a missing final line break (or the CR in CRLF) as a difference; only /